Pixel Snapper by Sprite Fusion (Hugo Duprez), MIT licence (LICENSE): https://github.com/Hugo-Dz/spritefusion-pixel-snapper
Built to WebAssembly from commit 02f9315 by tools/build-snapper.sh (wasm-bindgen 0.2.105, --target web), with our patch
(tools/pixel-snapper.patch):
 - edges are measured where a new pixel starts (a forward difference), so cuts no longer land a pixel short;
 - the grid is found by a lattice fit instead of upstream's step guess and walker: one square pixel size for both axes and
   a phase per axis, chosen by how much of the image's edge energy the grid lines explain minus a cost per line, then
   each cut nudged to the strongest edge within a fifth of a pixel. Fractional sizes (3.2 image pixels per art pixel, as AI
   art has) work, and a given --pixel-size is followed exactly.
On a benchmark of 262 images (synthetic sprites at 2-8 px per art pixel, clean / blurred / JPEG / jittered, plus the
games' AI sheets) it finds the right grid in 115 against upstream's 20 when guessing, and 197 against 63 when given the
size. Guessing stays unreliable on real AI sheets (they have no single exact grid): give the pixel size.
It snaps AI-made "pixel art" to the grid it was meant to have and to a palette: process_image(pngBytes, colours?,
pixelSize?, paletteHex?) → PNG bytes. Used by the PIXEL LAB page (portal/lab/) and tools/snap.mjs.
